Hi, everyone.
The goal of supervised machine learning
is often to develop a model to describe
the relationship between some input and output data set.
Then we want to be able to apply our trained model to an unseen
data set and predict what the outputs may be.
And a common question in this field
is, how do we actually train a model in practice?
How do we derive a model from collective data
and use it to perform classification or regression
in real life?
And in this recitation, we will go over that question.
And to start we will elaborate on the goal
when developing a supervised machine learning model.
Then we will go into how that model is
derived through the minimization of an objective function.
And to demonstrate this, we will use a support vector machine
example, and go over what the loss term and regularization
terms mean for the support vector machine.
Then we will talk about the hyperparameter
in the objective function, which balances these two terms
and talk about how the choice of hyperparameter
affects the performance of our model.
Following that, we will discuss cross-validation,
which is a technique that's used to actually find
the value of the hyperparameter that we should use in practice
using training data only.
And finally, we will go through a practical code example
together using scikit-learn to train a simple SVM classifier,
so that by the end of this recitation
you should be able to elaborate on any of these five topics
and be able to train a actual model on your own in practice.
So let's get started.
