In this video, let's go
over the process of cross-validation, which
is how we actually select a best value for our hyperparameter
alpha with training data only.
So the idea of cross-validation is simply that when we have
a training data set--
this is x data, y data--
we can break up this data set and only use part of it
for the actual training of our model
and use the other part as a validation of the model
that we trained using the training portion.
So this will be the validation set.
And the idea is that because we didn't use this portion
to train our model, this portion acts
as a test data set that we can use to fine tune what are
hyperparameter alpha should be.
And in practice, the thing that defines
the cross-validation process is that we have a parameter N that
determines how many times we segment our training data,
the entire training data set.
So in this example let's set N equal to 5.
The actual number of N varies depending on how much data
we have.
We Want to make sure that if we separate this entire training
data set into five different segments,
each segment is still large enough
that it's a representative sample of x data and y data.
So in this example, let's set N equal to 5.
Then what we do is we choose a value of alpha.
So usually we do alpha as a range, so starting with alpha 1
to alpha N where alpha 1 is less than alpha 2
is less than alpha N.
So which we test a bunch of alphas
and we start with alpha 1, and what we do
is that we take our entire training data set.
Here because N equal to 5, we segmented
into five random samples.
And then we iterate through different partitions
of these five segments.
So for iteration 1, we will use, let's say, the first four
segments as our training data and then
the last segment as the validation set, which is
essentially the test data set.
And because we have a fixed value of alpha here--
let's just make this clear--
because we have a fixed value of alpha here,
we can calculate how our model trained in this setting
actually performs on this testing set,
so we have some value of accuracy.
Let's call the accuracy S1 of alpha 1.
Now let's go into iteration two.
Here we would now switch up this segmentation
and we will say maybe use this first part
as our testing data set and the rest of our data
as our training set.
And from this we can, again, calculate accuracy
because alpha still fixed as alpha 1.
And let's call this S2 of alpha 1.
And we'll repeat this for five times
because here N is set to five.
So maybe on the fifth try, our testing data
will be this portion and our training data will be the rest.
And our accuracy will be S5 of alpha 1.
So now we see that for this value of alpha, alpha 1,
we have, in this case, five different accuracy values.
Let's just average that to give a accuracy
value to this particular value of alpha.
So let's call it S of alpha 1 is just 1 over N sum of S1 and SN
of alpha 1.
So now we have a idea of how well our model performs
on unseen testing data, if we set alpha to equal to alpha 1.
And of course, we'll repeat this process for each alpha in alpha
1 to alpha N. And this will give us then a function, let's say,
big S of alpha that consists of the accuracy
score for each of these alphas.
And our goal then is to now find the max of S of alpha.
So we want to find the alpha, which is alpha star, that
maximizes this function.
And this alpha star is then the alpha
we choose to actually train our model using our training data
set, so that we can expect to have the best performance
on unseen testing set.
So this is the process of cross-validation.
